+G5's independent starter/adapter work, G6 preparation/authorized discovery,
+G7 recruitment and permitted public review can overlap earlier work.
+Do not run a supposedly final campaign against a moving protocol.
+Post-R1 operations in plan section 10 begin after release; they are not extra
+prerequisites invented for today's candidate.
+
+## 5. Decisions that block implementation or a release claim
+
+Resolve these in the existing contract documents, one owner decision at a time.
+Do not ask agents to invent the answer in parallel.
+
+1. **Launch and validation authority:** today's intended claim; responsive POSIX
+   route; publication authorization if using GitHub CI; named accountable owners.
+2. **Recovery and starter reconciliation:** how ownership is proven before any
+   conditional restore, and authority for orphan credentials/historically partial
+   databases. Preserve unknown bytes and existing users until decided.
+3. **Six coupled protocol choices:** exact bridge/target/rollback artifacts;
+   finite reference inventory/lifecycle; recovery-set deletion interface;
+   QLT-11 unknown posture; evidence ceilings/no-truncation behavior; dedicated
+   migration route. Use `protocol-prototype.md`, `schema-migration.md`,
+   `config-completion.md` and the existing decision packet as the working set.
+4. **Complete capacity and activation:** present final wired sizes, omissions
+   and simplifications; obtain independent integrated review; approve exact
+   limits only if needed; obtain separate implementation/schema activation
+   sign-off. A partial candidate's overage is not an approved new budget.
+5. **Remaining feature contracts and canonical defects:** finding lifecycle,
+   demo/capture/export and run-directory exit semantics retain their specific
+   sign-off or spec-conflict disposition. No unilateral reason-code change.
+6. **Qualification and release:** authorize each native spike/campaign; approve
+   support tuple and model claims; record each Should disposition, any allowed
+   waiver and withdrawn/disabled capability; finalize policy contacts and
+   product/release approvals. Urgency does not waive the non-waivable suites.
+
+Existing cuts save work already: no committed-tree check, no external-CI import,
+manual recipe migration guidance instead of upgrades, and no remote `publish`
+verb. Do not reimplement those to improve checkbox counts. Manual guidance now
+exists locally; native adherence and the corresponding Should disposition are
+still separate work.
+
+## 6. Staffing and execution controls
+
+- **One integration/safety owner** owns pipeline, schema, store, ports, counters,
+  configuration identity and recovery changes. Reviewers are independent, not
+  simultaneous competing writers in these files.
+- **One starter/adapter owner** handles independently owned platform/tests and
+  real runtime characterization. Share stable APIs before editing common owners.
+- **One QA/qualification owner**, with UX/evaluation and release-owner decisions,
+  runs exact-artifact acceptance, evidence review and external coordination.
+  Agent output cannot replace a required human or second-maintainer role.
+
+These are recommended responsibilities, not newly assigned people or launched
+sessions. Two disjoint implementation streams plus one integration/validation
+stream are preferable to many agents repeatedly touching the central protocol.
+External participant access is a separate constraint, not solved by staffing
+more coding agents.
+
+At each integrated capability: failing distinguishing tests -> minimal repair ->
+focused real-boundary acceptance -> independent disposition -> affected fast
+gates. At each coherent candidate: full supported `npm run ci`, built
+dual-runtime UAT, exact evidence, then freeze. Run independent OS jobs in
+parallel, but do not compete heavyweight full suites on the same host.
+Any later runtime/payload change creates a new artifact; retain earlier evidence
+as history and rerun the gates affected by the change before acceptance.
+The final release artifact needs its own complete gates.
+
+Use the plan and acceptance worklist, not accumulating chat logs, as the handoff.
+For each package report: implemented, focused-accepted, supported-accepted,
+host-observed and formally-qualified separately, plus exact open obligation,
+owner and next dependency. Do not sum overlapping tests or call these columns
+interchangeable percentages.
+
+## 7. Release gate and schedule confidence
+
+**Unmodified public R1 is go only when all of the following hold:**
+
+- Every due Must passes on every supported tuple; every Should passes or has its
+  own signed omission; unresolved failures, non-applicability and residual risks
+  are public.
+- AC-07, AC-11, AC-16, AC-17, AC-25, AC-27 and AC-28 pass without exception.
+  Owner approval or an "R1 limited" label cannot waive them.
+- Native qualification, model/campaign and human evidence meet the canonical
+  gates, including the qualification cohort's two-week escaped-defect follow-up.
+- Applicable solo-maintainer changes complete the public 72-hour open-PR review
+  window; agent reviews do not count as a second maintainer. A release-significant
+  change is assessed against that policy, not grandfathered by an unrelated PR.
+- Exact-source supported CI, built UAT, privacy checks, independent rebuild,
+  provenance/signature/install verification, policy/support documentation and
+  product-owner/release-owner approvals are complete.
+
+Any allowed limited release follows PRD 4.7: explicit owner disposition,
+withdrawn claims and a disabled affected capability, not a warning hiding active
+unsafe behavior. No such waiver or implementation is supplied by this plan.
+
+**High confidence:** today's bounded schedule will expose blocking decisions
+early, prioritize data preservation, and avoid spending another day increasing
+test counts without completing a capability. This is confidence in sequencing
+and acceptance criteria, not in an unobserved repair or delivery date.
+
+**Not defensible yet:** a fixed hour/day promise for completing G1-G5. The
+partial candidate has missing mechanisms, unresolved policy and no complete fit.
+Re-estimate after the first stable complete protocol checkpoint, using observed
+end-to-end throughput and named external appointments, not earlier helper-test
+throughput.
+
+If the required qualification follow-up has not started, normal R1 cannot
+finish today: the two-week follow-up is elapsed time, not coding effort. The
+applicable 72-hour review can overlap other work but cannot be compressed.
+Those are conditional lower bounds, not a promise of release in exactly two
+weeks. Today's honest success is a safe, exact accepted candidate and a dated
+remaining critical path; if those gates fail, the honest result is no-go.
