+- **Unrecorded pre-migration data (recipe 0.4.6, local):** backup admission also checks for existing non-internal user tables, not only applied migration rows. Thus the first migration cannot bypass the existing data-preservation guarantee by starting from an empty history. Empty user tables are conservatively copied as well. Backup and restore exclude only the literal `sqlite_` internal prefix (`GLOB`, not `LIKE` with a wildcard underscore); `sqliteNotes` remains a user table and cannot bypass unrecorded-history refusal. A backup failure stops before migration SQL/history insertion; existing ledger initialization and SQLite housekeeping are not rolled back. The verified copy preserves original rows with empty history and remains subject to restore's unrecorded-schema refusal; this does not approve schema adoption, invent migration history or qualify concurrent writers.
 - **Restore** (`npm run db:restore -- <file>`): (1) server and restore tool both take `<db>.owner` by exclusive create (process ID and start time; dead holders removed under Tenet's liveness rule), because a WAL database cannot prove ownership; (2) validate the backup (integrity; schema compatible; not the live database or its alias); (3) write `<db>.restore-in-progress` naming where the originals move, then move the database and its `-wal` and `-shm` aside; (4) copy the backup in, open and verify; (5) remove the marker, and only then may originals be deleted. While the marker exists the server refuses to start and the tool offers finish or roll back; a test kills the restore at every step.
+- **Initially absent restore rollback (recipe 0.4.6, local):** new markers record original database presence. Rollback to recorded absence moves any current database/log files to the marker's existing kept-aside paths and reports them, preserving changed bytes rather than assuming they still match the backup. Preflight every live/aside collision before moving a file; keep the marker on refusal and resume partial suffix moves on retry. An unreadable marker or an older marker without enough information to establish the original state refuses rollback rather than falsely reporting restoration; a readable older marker can still finish using its verified backup. Retained files require operator inspection/cleanup. Real SQLite/direct-Node killed-process controls cover absence at marked/moved/copied/verified steps, changed bytes, collision, partial retry and refusal. These are local Windows observations, not POSIX/native qualification; the stale-owner race remains open.
 
 **ASVS applicability inventory (recipe 0.4.3, local):** `docs/asvs.md` accounts for every official v5.0.0 English Level 1 ID using pinned source/set digests, a method, explicit result and scoped applicability rationale. The initial result is 46 pending, 19 scoped non-applicable and five gaps, with no passing requirement or conformance claim. Completeness/reference controls and negative mutations prove inventory integrity only. The actual scaffold/finish fixture preserves the exact document and tracks it in Git; generated features need their own updated results. Cookie/development-identity/initial-secret/common-password/stewardship gaps remain open, not waived or silently changed. QLT-02 and independent/manual qualification remain incomplete.
 
+- **Restore marker admission (recipe 0.4.6, local):** parse marker contents as a typed object with nonempty backup/aside strings, finite nonnegative timestamp and optional boolean original presence. Invalid JSON values, including `null` and false-like scalars, remain pending/unreadable instead of being treated as absence. Startup and new restores refuse; recovery refuses before owner writes, retaining all files. Legacy objects without original presence remain accepted for the existing conservative recovery rules. One failing-first ten-value real-SQLite control plus all26restore controls pass locally; malformed marker admission does not qualify arbitrary paths or filesystem/owner races.
+
+- **Rollback crash prefixes (recipe 0.4.6, local):** the child fixture completes each real database/sidecar rename, synchronously reports the reached boundary, then kills itself. Retry after each of three prefixes preserves every original database and fixture-sidecar byte, restores absence and removes the marker last. All29restore controls pass on Windows24.22.1/22.12.0; this is not live-WAL, POSIX or the complete durability/race qualification matrix.
+- **Cold-start/browser implementation follow-through (recipe 0.4.6, local):** owner acquisition creates database parent directories before exclusive owner-file creation, without opening the database. Playwright quotes the actual Node executable path. A nested-directory ownership control and actual source-app/browser runs distinguish the prior startup failures. Current129unit/integration and12browser journeys pass locally on Windows24.22.1/22.12.0 with the starter's two-worker default; independent OpenAPI repeatability processes run concurrently without increasing the deadline. Windows forced fixture teardown leaves generated output/stale owner records, so these runs establish neither clean teardown nor the existing stale-owner/process-reconciliation guarantees.
+- **Owner-record admission (recipe 0.4.6, local):** parse existing owner JSON as an exact positive safe-integer PID/string-start record. Unreadable/invalid entries refuse and preserve the record/database instead of being reclaimed as dead. Only `ESRCH` establishes PID absence; unexpected signal errors propagate, and unavailable recorded/current start identity cannot prove replacement of a live PID. Real filesystem controls cover malformed JSON/value records, unreadable directory entries and live/dead holders. This does not close stale-owner replacement/release races or qualify POSIX process identity.
+- **Busy WAL reader admission (recipe 0.4.6, local):** a WAL-mode exclusive transaction does not exclude readers, and `wal_checkpoint(TRUNCATE)` can return busy without throwing. New restores require its first result column to be zero before declaring validation complete, creating the marker or moving files, otherwise report the existing database-in-use refusal. A real pinned-reader regression preserves the filename set, WAL/backup bytes and reader/writer snapshots; actual separate npm restore refuses while held and succeeds after release on Windows 24.22.1/22.12.0. Checkpointing may change main DB/shm bytes even on refusal. This point-in-time preflight does not exclude external clients opening afterward, repair stale-owner races or establish interrupted-recovery exclusion.
+- **Backup alias identity (recipe 0.4.6, local):** the existing live-database/sidecar alias refusal checks resolved names plus bigint filesystem device/file identities before reading the backup. Hard links have different resolved names but the same file identity. A failing-first real database-link control, candidate synthetic-sidecar controls and actual separate npm restore processes establish refusal with exact file/byte preservation and successful independent-backup retry on Windows 24.22.1/22.12.0. This is point-in-time identity, not atomic admission against concurrent path replacement or live-sidecar race qualification.
